The crack: \(V\) throws information away
Bring it down to one dimension, where we can see everything. On the line, what is the variety of \(x\)? The single point \(0\). What is the variety of \(x^2\)? Also just \(0\) — squaring does not move where a polynomial is zero, only how flatly it sits there. And \(x^3\), and \(x^{100}\), all of them vanish at \(0\) and nowhere else.

from sympy import symbols, solveset, S

x = symbols('x')
for f in (x, x**2, x**3):
    print(f"zeros of {f}: {solveset(f, x, domain=S.Complexes)}")

zeros of x: {0}
zeros of x**2: {0}
zeros of x**3: {0}


Three different polynomials, one identical zero set. So the map \(V\) is many-to-one: it cannot tell \(x\) from \(x^2\), because it only records where a polynomial vanishes and forgets to what order. The instant a map is many-to-one, it cannot be invertible, and our dream of a perfect round trip is in trouble. Take a moment to sit with that, because the fix is going to come from naming precisely what got forgotten.
What did \(V\) forget? It forgot powers. The polynomials \(x, x^2, x^3, \dots\) all collapse to the same shape, and the thing they have in common is that some power of each lands on the others: \(x^2\) is a power of \(x\), and conversely \(x\) is the “root” that \(x^2\) came from. If we want to undo \(V\), we will have to agree to treat a polynomial and its roots as the same word. That agreement has a name.

Fourier inversion turns the line into time
Fourier inversion says, under standard regularity hypotheses, that a function can be reconstructed from its frequency data:
\[
g_c(t)
=\lim_{T\to\infty}\frac{1}{2\pi}
  \int_{-T}^{T}\widehat{g_c}(\omega)e^{i\omega t}\,d\omega.
\tag{5}\]
For example, this pointwise formula holds when \(g_c\) is sufficiently smooth and integrable; at a jump it returns the midpoint of the two one-sided limits. There are broader \(L^2\) and distributional versions, but the mechanism is the same [2].
Substitute Equation 3 into Equation 5. For \(t&gt;0\), \(g_c(t)=e^{-ct}f(t)\), so
\[
f(t)
=\lim_{T\to\infty}\frac{e^{ct}}{2\pi}
  \int_{-T}^{T}F(c+i\omega)e^{i\omega t}\,d\omega.
\tag{6}\]
Now put \(s=c+i\omega\), so \(ds=i\,d\omega\) and \(e^{ct}e^{i\omega t}=e^{st}\). The horizontal frequency integral becomes a vertical contour integral:
\[
\boxed{
f(t)
=\frac{1}{2\pi i}
\lim_{T\to\infty}
\int_{c-iT}^{c+iT}e^{st}F(s)\,ds
}.
\tag{7}\]
This is the Bromwich inversion formula. Under one convenient classical set of conditions, \(f\) is continuous, \(f'\) is piecewise continuous, \(f\) satisfies Equation 4, and \(c&gt;\alpha\) [1].
The symbols can make Equation 7 look like a new machine. It is the machine we already had:

multiply \(f(t)\) by \(e^{-ct}\) so the future becomes integrable;
take its Fourier transform along the line \(c+i\omega\);
perform Fourier synthesis;
multiply by \(e^{ct}\) to undo the damping.

Laplace inversion is Fourier inversion with a movable exponential safety margin.
